   "title": "`ANCHOR_PATTERN` matches any tag starting with `<a` and `containsHtml` is evaluated after stripping anchors",
   "body": "`ANCHOR_PATTERN` is `</?a[^>]*>`, which matches not only `<a>` and `</a>` tags, but any HTML tag whose name begins with `a` (such as `<applet>`, `<audio>`, `<area>`, `<article>`, or `<aside>`), as well as uppercase or mixed-case tags if any are added. Because `santizeAnchors` runs on `englishValue` after `englishValue` is read, it does not modify `englishValue`, so `containsHtml(englishValue)` sees the original `<a ...>` in `englishValue` and selects `POLICY_SOME_HTML` even when the English string only contained an anchor tag and no `<br>`, `<p>`, `<strong>`, or `<b>` tags, allowing translations to inject `<p>`, `<br>`, `<b>`, or `<strong>` where the English message only had a link. Conversely, any non-anchor tag starting with `a` in a translation will be treated as an anchor tag by `santizeAnchors`. The regex should require a word boundary or whitespace/`>` after `a` (e.g. `</?a(?:\\s[^>]*)?>`), and `englishValue` should also have its matched anchors removed before `containsHtml(englishValue)` is checked."
  },
  {
   "index": 1,
   "file": "misc/theme-verifier/src/main/java/org/keycloak/themeverifier/VerifyMessageProperties.java",
   "line": 95,
   "severity": "high",
   "category": "security",
   "title": "Unescaping HTML entities after sanitization allows entity-encoded HTML/XSS payloads to bypass verification",
   "body": "In `normalizeValue` (line 130), `StringEscapeUtils.unescapeHtml4(value)` converts HTML entities in the raw property value into literal characters before sanitization, and line 95 calls `StringEscapeUtils.unescapeHtml4(sanitized)` on the sanitizer's output before comparing `sanitized` with `value`. If a translation contains entity-encoded dangerous markup such as `&lt;script&gt;alert(1)&lt;/script&gt;` or `&lt;img src=x onerror=alert(1)&gt;`, `normalizeValue` decodes it to `<script>...</script>`, the OWASP sanitizer strips `<script>`, and the comparison catches it. However, if a translation contains a character or tag that the OWASP sanitizer *escapes* rather than strips (for example, unrecognized numeric/custom tags like `<1>` or text following `<` that OWASP turns into `&lt;...`), `unescapeHtml4(sanitized)` turns `&lt;` back into `<`, making `sanitized` equal to `value` again and completely defeating the sanitizer check for any construct that OWASP neutralizes via entity encoding instead of element removal."
  },
  {
   "index": 2,
   "file": "misc/theme-verifier/src/main/java/org/keycloak/themeverifier/VerifyMessageProperties.java",
   "line": 107,
   "severity": "medium",
   "category": "bug",
   "title": "`StringIndexOutOfBoundsException` when `value` and `sanitized` share a prefix and suffix that overlap",
   "body": "When computing the diff slice between `value` and `sanitized`, `start` is incremented from the front and `end` is incremented from the back independently without ensuring `start + end <= Math.min(value.length(), sanitized.length())`. If one string is a repetition/insertion inside identical surrounding characters (for example `value = \"<b><b>\"` and `sanitized = \"<b>\"`, where `start` reaches 3 and `end` reaches 3 while `sanitized.length()` is 3), `sanitized.substring(start, sanitized.length() - end)` calls `substring(3, 0)` and throws `StringIndexOutOfBoundsException`, crashing the verifier instead of reporting the illegal HTML message. The second loop condition should guard `start + end < sanitized.length() && start + end < value.length()`."
  },
  {
   "index": 3,
   "file": "js/apps/account-ui/maven-resources/theme/keycloak.v3/account/messages/messages_en.properties",
